sum='Ullal, a small principality on the west coast of Karnataka, had been captured by the Portuguese and had to pay them tribute as a vassal. On her deathbed, the old queen grieved; her daughter Abbakka Devi promised to drive the Portuguese out of the west coast. Crowned queen, Abbakka asked her people for whole-hearted co-operation. She told her husband Veera Narasimha, king of Bangadi, that she would stop paying tribute and stop selling spices and cloth to the Portuguese. He thought it rash and dangerous for himself and ordered soldiers to seize her, but no soldier moved – they followed their brave queen. The other rajas refused to unite, saying they were no match for the Portuguese, which only strengthened her resolve to fight alone. She told the Portuguese envoy, “We are a sovereign country. We don’t pay tribute to Portugal.” She built merchant ships and, allied with the Zamorin of Kozhikode, traded profitably with Arabia. When the Portuguese captured her commander’s ship and then attacked, she unleashed her forces and captured the Portuguese fort at Mangalore; the commander Ribero escaped. The angry Portuguese governor of Goa sent a large force against Ullal; the battle raged for four days, but Abbakka’s forces won and the Portuguese retreated, never to trouble Ullal again. Later she helped Venkatappa Nayak of Keladi take the fort of Mangalore.',
